            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "-oso in English? (-ous.) And -ción? (-tion.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more, and it is the one that makes ideas into things."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: a quality, made into a thing",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish -ncia is English -nce."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin -entia turned a quality into a noun. If something differs, the state of differing is differentia. Spanish kept the ending nearly whole; English took it through French, where it wore down to -nce."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Spanish",
                "English"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 5,
              "utterances": [
                "diferencia means difference.",
                "importancia means importance.",
                "experiencia means experience.",
                "distancia means distance.",
                "paciencia means patience."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One word wearing two coats, five times over. This is the same shape as -ción and -tion: not a resemblance, the same ending arriving by two roads."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: what you do with it",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Every -ncia word is a noun, and every one is feminine — so the article never surprises you:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la diferencia, la importancia, una experiencia."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And you already know what goes after a noun:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "una diferencia grande — a big difference."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That sentence uses three things you were taught separately: a noun built by an ending, an article chosen by gender, and a describing word in its Spanish position. None of it was memorised as a phrase."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One that will look wrong, and is not."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ciencia is science, not cience. The ending behaved exactly as promised — it is the root that English spells differently, because scientia came into English through a French spelling that kept the sc-."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So when a decoded word looks almost right, trust the ending and check the root. Almost right is usually right."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
